# Spectral Analysis in LIBS

## Overview

This section describes the spectroscopic analysis of optical emission produced by laser-induced plasma during the LIBS measurements.

The analysis focused on identifying characteristic elemental emission lines and examining spectral features relevant to the compositional analysis of Cu–Al2O3 composite materials.

## Emission Line Identification

Characteristic emission lines were investigated using reference data from the NIST Atomic Spectra Database.

The identification process involved:

- Examination of recorded optical emission spectra.
- Identification of characteristic spectral lines.
- Comparison of observed wavelengths with NIST reference data.
- Evaluation of spectral features associated with the investigated material.

## Spectral Behavior

The recorded emission spectra were examined in terms of:

- Spectral line positions.
- Relative emission intensities.
- Line profiles and spectral features.
- Changes in emission behavior.

## Self-Absorption Effects

Self-absorption was considered as an important factor affecting measured emission line intensities.

Its influence was investigated in the context of quantitative LIBS analysis and compositional assessment.

## Material System

The spectral analysis was associated with Cu–Al2O3 composite materials, including samples with an investigated Al2O3 concentration of approximately 1.1 wt%.

## Analysis Scope

This document provides an overview of the spectroscopic analysis approach used in the MSc research.

Experimental spectra, wavelength assignments, and quantitative analysis results will be included where available.
